carbon dioxide or CO2 emissions soared  to a record level last year by several  indexes Qatar ranks high globally when  it comes to CO2 emissions per capita  this November the gulf nation is  pledging to host the first ever carbon  neutral World Cup  a lot of people will come to the Qatar  by air but the activities here within  Qatar is designed in such a way that all  the emissions associated  there will be a limited emissions and  this emissions will be balanced the  layout of stadiums over a small  geographic area means once fans land in  Qatar they'll travel a relatively short  distance by Metro Electric bus or car to  matches and stadiums built with  sustainability in mind  after football's Premier spectacle is  over the CO2 emissions during the 28  days will be offset by investing in  carbon credits  but a report by carbon market watch says  Qatar 2022's carbon neutral plan is  misleading and based on what it calls  creative calculations  it is highly unlikely that this world  cup is going to be cover neutral it is  not a credible claim and there is a big  risk that it's going to mislead the  public into thinking that this has no  impact on the climate when actually it  definitely does  the organizing committee says qatar's  historic Ambitions should be recognized  not criticized it points to the almost 1  million square meters of green space  created and a new solar power plant that  will generate renewable energy for years  after the tournament ends We Stand by  our planning We Stand by our  calculations and we stand by our plans  to offset what's remaining in the best  possible way with the best information  that we have  a team from Qatar University will be  setting up weather monitoring stations  and sharing the daily air quality with  fans during the World Cup  the hope is it will spread awareness  about the impact we have on the climate  and how to reduce our carbon footprint  Natasha gname Al Jazeera Doha
 

